6, 2027. The successful candidate will primarily teach undergraduate courses in American History (U.S. History I & II), and World History (Western Civilization I & II, and 20th-Century World History I & II), in addition to courses in the candidate's areas of expertise. The normal teaching load for this position is 9 credits per semester. The IAS department serves the General Education needs of the campus and houses interdisciplinary majors alongside colleagues in Philosophy, Literature, Communication, Anthropology, Sociology, Psychology, and Political Science. Faculty members are expected to pursue research and other scholarly work, and to engage in service activities. The successful candidate will also advise students and may contribute to student recruitment, course creation, curriculum design and reviews, and student success efforts. Required Qualifications: Earned PhD in History (or a closely related field). ABD candidates will be considered, with the degree conferred by July 1, 2027. Demonstrated ability to teach undergraduate survey courses in both American History and World History. Effective oral and written communication skills. Preferred Qualifications: Previous teaching experience in college level History courses. An established research program with peer reviewed publications and scholarly productivity at the university level. Previous experience with student success, advising, recruitment, and retention efforts. Ability to teach a broad range of undergraduate History courses including, but not limited to: History of Science & Technology History of Science, Technology, Medicine, & Public Health History of Nature, Society, & The Environment Economic & Labor History For full consideration application materials must be complete. Please include: Cover letter, which specifically addresses the above-mentioned qualifications. A statement detailing teaching philosophy and scholarly plans. Curriculum Vitae. Copies of unofficial graduate transcript Contact information for three professional references. Application Requirements For full consideration application materials must be complete. Any offer of employment is contingent upon a satisfactory criminal background check. Equal Opportunity Employer Statement Montana Tech is committed to providing a working and learning environment free from discrimination. As such, the University does not discriminate in the admission, access to or conduct of its educational programs and activities nor in its employment policies and practices on the basis of race, color, religion, national origin, ethnicity, creed, service in the uniformed services (as defined in state and federal law), veteran status, gender, age, political beliefs, marital or family status, pregnancy, physical or mental disability, genetic information, gender identity, gender expression, or sexual orientation or preference. In support of the University's mission to be inclusive and diverse, applications from qualified minorities, women, veterans and persons with disabilities are highly encouraged. Montana Tech makes accommodation for any known disability that may interfere with an applicant's ability to compete in the hiring process or an employee's ability to perform the duties of the job. In compliance with the Montana Veteran's Employment Preference Act, Montana Tech provides preference in employment to veterans, disabled veterans, and certain eligible relatives of veterans. Employment Eligibility: All New Employees must be eligible and show employment eligibility verification by the first date of employment at Montana Tech as legally required (e.g., Form I-9). Montana Tech will require proof of authorization to work in the United States before final hiring. Due to the September 19, 2025 Presidential Proclamation and related guidance from USCIS and the Department of State, the University may be limited in its ability to offer H-1B sponsorship. At this time, we are unable to sponsor new H-1B visas that require payment of